module gunbc.runner.runner_qualification_receipt

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }

// THIS RECEIPT WAS PUBLISHED WITH A FALSE JOIN AND IS REWRITTEN RATHER THAN QUIETLY CORRECTED.
//
// Its first revision claimed that host capture X was the host-side evidence for actions run
// 34090864879, a job that SUCCEEDED. The capture is a boot that fetched no credential, refused to
// start a runner, and panicked. Two different boots of the same image, joined on a string they both
// happened to carry.
//
// THE WRONG ARTIFACT SURVIVED THE MISTAKE AND IT IS THE MORE VALUABLE ONE. What that capture records
// is a fail-open: with a zero-byte credential the host created the JIT drive, created the workspace,
// and launched Firecracker anyway. That defect would not have appeared in the receipt this file was
// meant to be.

data receipt_subject_run: NonEmptyStr = "actions run 34090864879, conclusion success, dispatched with attempt=runner-canary-6"
data receipt_subject_build: NonEmptyStr = "runner-canary-6"

// ── the join that was false, and why the identifier could not have worked ──────────────────────

// "runner-canary-6" IS A BUILD LABEL, NOT AN ATTEMPT IDENTITY. Every power cycle of that image
// prints it, and the harness cycled up to four times looking for a boot it could observe. The
// concrete host attempt is runner-canary-6-83b96b35; the workflow input echoed only the build.
//
// ONE-SHOT CREDENTIAL DELIVERY MAKES THIS DECIDABLE AND THE RECEIPT STILL GOT IT WRONG. Exactly one
// boot of that build could ever have redeemed the credential, so a label shared by all of them can
// never name the one that did.
//
// AND THE WORKFLOW'S runs-on IS STILL [self-hosted, mtcollins], so the dispatch input is an assertion
// echoed by whatever runner takes the job rather than an assignment to one registration. An
// attempt-unique label on both the JIT registration and runs-on is what would make the binding real.
type AttemptJoin
  = JoinedByAttemptUniqueLabel { label: NonEmptyStr }
  | JoinedByEchoedBuildLabel { why_insufficient: NonEmptyStr }
  | JoinedByTimestamp

data qualification_join_standing: AttemptJoin = JoinedByEchoedBuildLabel {
  why_insufficient: "the build label is printed by every boot of the image and the job's runs-on matches any mtcollins runner, so the input records what the operator intended rather than which registration served the job",
}

// ── the host evidence for the successful run does not exist ────────────────────────────────────

// AN EARLIER CYCLE ENDED WITH "SOL session closed by BMC", AND THE HARNESS SCORED THAT AS A FAILED
// BOOT. A dropped CONSOLE is not a failed BOOT. That boot continued, fetched the one-shot credential,
// registered, and ran the job to success at 06:37:54 -- unobserved, because the only instrument had
// been disconnected by the BMC rather than by anything the host did.
//
// SO THE HARNESS POWER-CYCLED A MACHINE THAT WAS RUNNING A LIVE CI JOB. That is the operational
// defect behind the mislabelled file, and it is more serious than the label: an observer that cannot
// distinguish "I stopped watching" from "it stopped working" will keep destroying the runs it is
// meant to record.
//
// NO DIGEST RECOVERS WHAT WAS NEVER CAPTURED. The successful run's host evidence is gone, and this
// receipt claims none.
type HostEvidenceStanding
  = HostEvidenceCaptured { artifact: NonEmptyStr }
  | HostEvidenceLost { cause: NonEmptyStr, recoverable: Bool }

data qualification_host_evidence: HostEvidenceStanding = HostEvidenceLost {
  cause: "the console session for the boot that ran the job was closed by the BMC, and the harness then power-cycled that host believing it had failed to boot",
  recoverable: false,
}

// ── what the GitHub side does establish on its own ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// THESE ARE GUEST-SIDE FACTS AND THE JOB LOG IS A COMPETENT WITNESS TO THEM. They do not depend on
// any host capture, which is why they survive the loss above.
data job_side_facts: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "Runner name: mtcollins-1, Machine name: gunbc-mtcollins-1",
  "runner-version: 2.337.0, read from the guest's own Runner.Listener rather than from a service banner -- the release gunbc_runner_guest_image now pins",
  "the attempt workspace mounted with 3827 MB free, and the job wrote 16 MiB, read the size back and deleted it",
  "the BMC was unreachable from inside the job",
]

// AND THE HOST-SIDE HALF OF EACH IS MISSING FOR THIS RUN. The counters that corroborated the BMC
// denial in gunbc.runner.runner_first_job_receipt have no counterpart here, because the capture that
// would carry them is the one that was lost.
data job_side_facts_uncorroborated: NonEmptyStr = "every row above is guest-side only for this run; the host-side corroboration exists for run 34086151842 and not for this one"

// ── the fail-open the wrong capture revealed ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE HOST OBSERVED A ZERO-BYTE CREDENTIAL AND STARTED A VIRTUAL MACHINE ANYWAY. It created the JIT
// drive from an empty file, formatted a workspace, and launched Firecracker; the refusal happened
// inside the guest, after the VM existed. A missing credential is a condition the host can decide
// before spending anything, and it decided nothing.
//
// THE ARM MUST REFUSE BEFORE THE LAUNCH, NOT INSIDE THE GUEST. Every terminal case below owes ZERO
// Firecracker launches, because a VM started on an inadmissible envelope has already spent the
// resources the refusal exists to protect and has already widened the surface.
type JitEnvelopeAdmission
  = JitEnvelopeAdmitted { payload_digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | JitEnvelopeRefusedBeforeLaunch { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | JitEnvelopeUncheckedAndLaunched { observed: NonEmptyStr }

data jit_admission_as_observed: JitEnvelopeAdmission = JitEnvelopeUncheckedAndLaunched {
  observed: "jitconfig-bytes=0 and the host proceeded to build the drive, format the workspace and launch the VMM; the guest refused afterwards",
}

data jit_refusal_cases_owing_zero_launches: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "no payload fetched",
  "empty payload",
  "payload outside declared size bounds",
  "malformed envelope",
  "envelope bound to a different physical unit",
  "envelope bound to a different host boot",
  "envelope bound to a different VM attempt",
  "expired envelope",
  "signature verification failure",
  "replayed envelope",
]

// ── four outcomes that are not one outcome ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// FIRECRACKER EXITED 0 ON THE BOOT WHERE NOTHING RAN. The guest refused its credential, exited as
// PID 1, panicked, and the VMM reaped it and reported success -- because the VMM's contract is about
// the machine it ran, not about the work inside it.
//
// SO A CLEAN VMM EXIT MUST NEVER COLLAPSE INTO AN ATTEMPT SUCCEEDING. These are four separate
// readings and this lane has already conflated the outer ones once.
data distinct_outcomes: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "the VMM ran and exited cleanly",
  "the guest reached userspace",
  "the runner registered and listened",
  "a GitHub job ran and reported",
]

// ── the retirement observation, which IS sound and belongs to the failed boot ──────────────────

// THIS EVIDENCE IS REAL AND ITS SUBJECT IS THE CREDENTIAL-LESS BOOT. Retirement after a guest that
// refused is a weaker test than retirement after a job that ran -- there is less to clean up -- so
// it is recorded against the attempt that produced it rather than borrowed by the successful one.
data retirement_observed_on_failed_boot: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "jail-removed=yes",
  "post-jit-device-present=no",
  "post-workspace-present=no",
  "post-jailbase-entries=0 and post-jailbase-mounted=0",
  "post-cgroup-empty=yes then post-cgroup-removed=yes",
  "post-tap-present=no, post-ruleset-present=no, post-nft-process=0",
  "surviving-firecracker=0, surviving-jailer=0",
]

data retirement_after_real_work_unproven: NonEmptyStr = "retirement is observed after a boot whose guest refused immediately; retirement after a boot that actually ran a job, wrote a workspace and held a registration is not observed by this receipt"

// ── three instrument defects, and the one that matters most ────────────────────────────────────

data instrument_defects_found: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "the harness cannot distinguish a console the BMC dropped from a host that failed to boot, and its response to the ambiguity is a power cycle -- so its failure mode is destroying the run it is observing",
  "the absence checks ran before the unmount that produces the absence, so they were permanently YES and briefly read as a credential leak",
  "the capture's terminal condition matched a line printed before cleanup, so a retirement receipt ended three lines short of its own evidence",
  "wc -c reported 0 bytes for a sparse 4 GiB image that mke2fs had formatted and a guest mounted with 3827 MB free",
]

// THE FIRST ONE IS NOT A LOGGING BUG. An observer whose ambiguous reading triggers a destructive
// action is the absorbing-fallback shape: it cannot tell two states apart, so it picks the arm that
// makes the ambiguity go away, and the cost lands on whatever was actually running.
data harness_defect_class: NonEmptyStr = "a dropped console and a failed boot are different states; treating them alike and power-cycling is a destructive action taken on an unresolved reading"

// ── the extraction claim, tempered ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE COMMITTED EXTRACTS CARRY THE FULL CAPTURE'S DIGEST AND CUT LINE, AND AN EARLIER REVISION
// CALLED THAT "checkable". It is not: the full bytes are not committed, so a reader can verify an
// extract against its own digest but cannot re-cut it from a source they do not have. The digest
// identifies the source; it does not make the extraction reproducible.
data extraction_standing: NonEmptyStr = "the extract is content-addressed and the unavailable source capture is identified by digest; independent re-derivation of the cut would require publishing the full capture"

// ── standing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

data qualification_standing: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "OPEN: exact job-to-host-attempt binding -- needs an attempt-unique runner label on both the JIT registration and runs-on",
  "OPEN: pre-launch refusal of an inadmissible JIT envelope, with zero Firecracker launches on every terminal case",
  "OPEN: an authenticated, attempt-bound JIT envelope; neither requester nor responder is authenticated today",
  "OPEN: a harness that distinguishes a lost console from a failed boot and never power-cycles on the ambiguity",
  "OPEN: runtime artifact mismatch prevention -- boot_probe_verdict is still unwired",
  "OPEN: two attempts on one host boot, checkout, a JS action, a toolchain step, metered vCPU-seconds",
  "CLOSED for this run, guest-side only: the pinned runner version and a writable attempt workspace",
]
